# Site configurations

Every site the user hands over gets an entry in `sites/sites.json`, shown read-only in the app's **Sites** panel. The server matches a queued URL's host against `match` (domain or subdomain) and passes `ytDlpArgs` to yt-dlp; `sites/plugins` is always passed with `--plugin-dirs`.

When given a URL, establish success yourself:

1. Probe without downloading: `curl -I`, then `yt-dlp --simulate` (full network permission).
2. Prove it with real media: download the file or a sample into a scratch folder under `/tmp` (never `~/Downloads`), check it with `ffprobe` and a full `ffmpeg -f null` decode, then delete it.
3. If it fails, fix in this order: update yt-dlp, per-site options (`ytDlpArgs`), the user's browser cookies (ask first), then a plugin in `sites/plugins/siphon/yt_dlp_plugins/extractor/` (yt-dlp ignores a `yt_dlp_plugins` folder placed directly in the plugin dir). The entry's `plugin` path is relative to `sites/plugins`.
4. Record the entry: `method` (`builtin` | `options` | `embed` | `plugin` | `unsupported`; `embed` entries also set `"followEmbed": true`, and the native app downloads the page's player iframe instead of the page), `status` (`working` | `broken` | `unsupported` | `untested`), `lastTested`, `testResult` (what the check showed), `notes` (why it failed and why the fix works), `exampleUrl`.
5. Do not circumvent DRM; mark such sites `unsupported` and say why.

## The app's persistent copy

- The native app keeps its own copy at `~/Library/Application Support/Siphon/sites/` (`sites.json` + `plugins/`), seeded from the bundled repo copy and merged on every launch: a repo entry replaces the entries it overlaps unless one of them has a later `lastTested`.
- When a link's site has no entry, the app tests it during the real download, tries automatic fixes (browser impersonation, referer, generic page scan, following the page's player iframe), checks the file with `ffprobe`, and records the result with `"source": "learned"`.
- Learned `broken` entries are the work list: fix them with the steps above (plugins and cookies are beyond the app), then record the fix in the repo's `sites/sites.json` with a newer `lastTested` so it wins the merge. Promote useful learned `working` entries the same way.
